BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (4. A person becomes angry during an argument, and both pulse and pressure rise. Which route can initiate this) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (response?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: A. Cortical signals acting through the hypothalamus on autonomic centres.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Emotions such as anger begin in the cortex. The cortex drives the hypothalamus, which excites the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (vasomotor centre, raising sympathetic output so heart rate and blood pressure rise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not B: Renal volume control is too slow and not the primary emotional route.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: The CSF pathways do not directly account for this autonomic response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: Platelet serotonin is a local vascular constrictor, not the main emotional signal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q57 | 30, 33) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (5. Why can vision fail before consciousness during strong positive acceleration?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: A. Retinal perfusion falls as blood shifts toward the feet.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: Positive g causes leg pooling and reduced pressure at eye level, so retinal perfusion can fail before) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (cerebral consciousness.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not B: Pressure at eye level decreases during positive g.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not C: The mechanism is reduced eye-level arterial perfusion, not an inevitable ICP excess.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: Headward blood movement occurs during negative g.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q16 | Circulation 3, slide 9; Chapter 19 ?6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (6. A patient's right ventricular pumping fails and right atrial pressure rises. Which consequence is most) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (likely?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: C. Reduced venous return gradient with congestion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: Higher right atrial pressure opposes venous return and transmits backward into systemic veins, causing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (congestion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not A: Higher right atrial pressure reduces the upstream-to-atrial gradient.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not B: Elevated atrial pressure tends to increase congestion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not D: JVP often becomes elevated rather than disappearing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q77 | 24, 97; Chapter 12 ?6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (7. Which combination correctly characterises isovolumetric ventricular contraction?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 324.1 Tm (Answer: A. All valves closed, ventricular pressure rises, volume fixed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 307.8 Tm (Explanation: Both ventricles generate rising pressure with all inlet and outlet valves closed, so neither receives) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (nor ejects blood.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.6 Tm (Not B: Open valves permit volume exchange.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.2 Tm (Not C: AV valves do not remain open once ventricular pressure rises above atrial.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.8 Tm (Not D: Falling pressure describes isovolumetric relaxation, not contraction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.4 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q19 | 14-15) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 215.2 Tm (8. A patient loses substantial albumin into urine during kidney disease. What Starling-force change is) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (expected?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 184.8 Tm (Answer: D. Reduced plasma oncotic pressure promotes oedema.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 168.5 Tm (Explanation: Proteinuria reduces circulating albumin and its inward oncotic force, favouring movement of fluid into) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 155.4 Tm (interstitium.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.3 Tm (Not A: Viscosity is not the principal mechanism of protein-loss oedema.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.9 Tm (Not B: Capillary permeability elsewhere need not decrease.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.5 Tm (Not C: Albumin loss lowers oncotic pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.1 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q88 | 93) Tj ET
