BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not B: Aortic body receptors monitor blood chemistry.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Not C: Carotid sinus receptors monitor high-pressure arterial stretch.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.0 Tm (Not D: Type A activity is linked to atrial systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.6 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q47 | 28) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (16. A standard ECG uses ten skin electrodes but produces twelve electrical views. How is this possible?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: B. Several leads are calculated from combinations of the same electrode voltages.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: A lead is an electrical viewpoint calculated from one or more electrode potentials, so ten physical) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (contacts can generate twelve different standard recordings.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not A: Routine twelve-lead ECG does not require intracardiac electrodes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not C: All twelve are electrical leads; pressure sensors do not generate extra leads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not D: The limb contacts remain in place during a standard recording.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q14 | 3-5; Chapter 6 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (17. Why does positioning an accelerating pilot more horizontally improve tolerance to positive g?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 324.1 Tm (Answer: A. It shortens the heart-to-brain hydrostatic column.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 307.8 Tm (Explanation: A more horizontal posture decreases the vertical distance over which acceleration reduces pressure) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (toward the head and brain.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.6 Tm (Not B: Increasing the vertical distance would worsen the gradient.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.2 Tm (Not C: Enhanced ankle pooling would reduce central venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.8 Tm (Not D: Greater dependent filtration would not protect brain perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.4 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q17 | Circulation 3, slide 11; Chapter 19 ?6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 215.2 Tm (18. Cardiac output falls by 20%, but total peripheral resistance rises by 25%. What happens to mean arterial) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (pressure in the simplified MAP relation?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 184.8 Tm (Answer: A. It remains approximately unchanged.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 168.5 Tm (Explanation: In MAP ~ CO x TPR, 0.80 x 1.25 = 1.00, so the changes cancel mathematically.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 152.4 Tm (Not B: The combined product does not fall by one quarter.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.0 Tm (Not C: That ignores the fall in cardiac output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.6 Tm (Not D: That ignores the compensatory rise in resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.2 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q19 | 1, 19-21) Tj ET
