BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 03 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (29. A small additional deflection appears after the T wave in an otherwise interpretable trace. Which) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (description fits the U wave?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. A possible late ventricular repolarisation phenomenon.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: The lecture describes a U wave, when seen after T, as the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (Its precise physiological origin is uncertain.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: Atrial depolarisation is the P wave preceding QRS.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: Rapid ventricular depolarisation is represented by QRS, not U.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: The ventricular plateau is associated with the ST segment.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q29 | 10) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (30. Which substance passes relatively readily across the intact blood-brain barrier without a carrier?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: C. Carbon dioxide.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: Small diffusible gases such as oxygen and carbon dioxide cross the endothelial membrane relatively) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (easily.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not A: Albumin is a large protein with restricted passage.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not B: Protein binding reduces free diffusion across the BBB.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: Large immunoglobulins do not easily cross intact endothelial tight junctions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q14 | 18-19) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (31. What is the main purpose of wide changes in skin blood flow during heating or cooling?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: C. To adjust body heat gain and loss.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: Cutaneous vessels dilate to increase heat loss and constrict to conserve heat; flow can far exceed) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 434.0 Tm (nutritional needs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.9 Tm (Not A: Skin metabolic demand is relatively modest compared with variation in perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.5 Tm (Not B: Heart pacemaking is not the main thermoregulatory role of skin circulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not D: Pulmonary-systemic pressure equality is unrelated to thermoregulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q46 | 39-41) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (32. Why does sustained systemic hypertension tend to raise myocardial oxygen demand?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 337.8 Tm (Answer: D. The ventricle must generate higher pressure for ejection.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 321.5 Tm (Explanation: Higher arterial pressure increases ventricular pressure work and wall stress, requiring more oxygen) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 308.4 Tm (for force generation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.3 Tm (Not A: Higher afterload generally increases wall stress.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.9 Tm (Not B: The myocardium continues to contract against the higher load.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not C: Coronary oxygen extraction remains substantial.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q48 | 54; Chapter 8 ?7) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.9 Tm (33. An echocardiogram shows poor ventricular contraction and a reduced ejection fraction. Which primary) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 215.2 Tm (failure pattern does this support?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 198.5 Tm (Answer: D. Systolic dysfunction with impaired ejection.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 182.2 Tm (Explanation: Systolic heart failure primarily reflects reduced contractility; a smaller proportion of) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 169.1 Tm (end-diastolic volume is ejected during systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 153.0 Tm (Not A: Stiff filling is the typical description of diastolic failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.6 Tm (Not B: Improved relaxation is not impaired systolic pumping.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.2 Tm (Not C: Vascular dilation alone does not define ventricular systolic failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.8 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q50 | Chapter 21 ?2 \(supplementary\)) Tj ET
