BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 03 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (34. Why is a distinct atrial repolarisation wave usually absent from a normal ECG?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: C. Its small electrical signal is obscured by the ventricular QRS complex.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: Atrial repolarisation occurs around the time of ventricular depolarisation; the much larger QRS) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (electrical signal masks it on the surface trace.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not A: Repolarisation does generate voltage differences, although the signal is small.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not B: Atrial repolarisation does not need to await the beginning of ventricular diastole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not D: That statement does not explain why the wave is not separately visible.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q25 | Chapter 6 ?4 \(approved-chapter explanation\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (35. A measured QRS occupies 3.5 small squares at 25 mm/s. Which statement is supported by the lecture's usual) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (duration range?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: B. It lasts 0.14 s and is longer than the stated usual range.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: At 25 mm/s, 3.5 small squares equal 3.5 x 0.04 = 0.14 s, above the lecture's usual QRS duration of) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (0.06-0.10 s.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not A: 0.10 s corresponds to 2.5 small squares at the standard speed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not C: 0.07 s incorrectly halves the 0.14-s measured duration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: 0.35 s does not follow from the standard 0.04-s small-square duration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q39 | 8, 13) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (36. A Wiggers diagram shows the QRS complex before a steep ventricular pressure rise. What does the QRS) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (represent?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: D. Electrical activation of the ventricles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: QRS reflects ventricular electrical activation; mechanical pressure rises after excitation, during) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (isovolumetric contraction. Thus QRS precedes the pressure rise; it does not measure ejection.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not A: Atrial filling is not the electrical event marked by QRS.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not B: Pulmonary veins do not close as cardiac valves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not C: Ventricular repolarisation is represented mainly by the T wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q24 | 10-17; Chapter 4 ?9) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (37. A patient becomes dizzy on standing after starting a sympatholytic drug. Which compensatory response is) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.1 Tm (most directly weakened?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 310.4 Tm (Answer: D. Constriction of systemic arterioles and veins.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.1 Tm (Explanation: Sympatholytic drugs blunt reflex arteriolar and venous constriction, so the circulation cannot) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.0 Tm (adequately oppose blood pooling and falling arterial pressure on standing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.9 Tm (Not A: Walking uses a mechanical muscle pump, not this autonomic drug pathway.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.5 Tm (Not B: Venous valve directionality is mechanical and not directly abolished by sympatholytic drugs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.1 Tm (Not C: Cerebral metabolic vasodilatation is not directly a sympathetic vasoconstrictor response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.7 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q15 | Circulation 3, slide 8; Chapter 19 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (38. A patient develops rapidly increasing intracranial pressure after a brain haemorrhage. What first) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 187.8 Tm (threatens cerebral perfusion?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.1 Tm (Answer: D. Compression of brain vessels and reduced driving pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.8 Tm (Explanation: Rising intracranial pressure compresses intracranial vessels and lowers cerebral perfusion pressure,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 141.7 Tm (risking medullary ischaemia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.6 Tm (Not A: Raised intracranial pressure does not require systemic blood loss.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.2 Tm (Not B: Dilation cannot prevent the loss of cerebral driving pressure as intracranial pressure rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 98.8 Tm (Not C: Perfusion initially becomes harder as intracranial pressure rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 85.4 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q53 | 31-32, 35-36) Tj ET
