BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 03 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (39. A vessel is 50 cm below heart level in an upright patient. Approximately how much hydrostatic pressure) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (does gravity add?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. About 39 mmHg.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Hydrostatic pressure changes by about 0.77 mmHg per centimetre of height; a 50-cm drop adds) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (approximately 39 mmHg.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: Nineteen mmHg corresponds to roughly 25 cm.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: Seventy-seven mmHg corresponds to roughly 100 cm.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: One hundred mmHg is much larger than the calculated gradient.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q1 | Circulation 3, slide 2; Chapter 19 ?1) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (40. What closes the aortic valve at the beginning of ventricular relaxation?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: C. Aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: When ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure, brief reversal tendency closes the aortic valve) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (and starts isovolumetric relaxation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not A: Venous pressure does not directly determine aortic valve closure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not B: Atrial pressure governs mitral opening, not aortic closure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: Ventricular pressure exceeding aortic pressure opens the valve.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q23 | 17) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (41. Which sequence best describes the classical triple response of Lewis?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: B. Red line, flare and wheal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: The classical triple response consists of local redness, a spreading flare and an oedematous wheal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 431.0 Tm (Not A: A white line is a separate lighter-stroke reaction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.6 Tm (Not C: Wheal formation requires local fluid leak rather than none.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not D: Thrombosis and scar are not normal features.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q52 | 40) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 367.6 Tm (42. Which condition is most likely to depress myocardial contractility rather than enhance it?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 350.9 Tm (Answer: C. Severe hypoxia with acidosis.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 334.6 Tm (Explanation: Severe hypoxia and acidosis impair myocardial function and are listed as causes of depressed inotropy.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 318.5 Tm (Not A: Beta-1 activation generally enhances contractility.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 305.1 Tm (Not B: Catecholamines usually raise inotropy initially.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 291.7 Tm (Not D: Increased cytosolic calcium generally enhances contraction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.3 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q41 | 48) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 255.1 Tm (43. A patient has had high arterial pressure for months. Why might baroreceptors fail to drive pressure back) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 241.4 Tm (to the original baseline?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.7 Tm (Answer: A. Their operating range resets around the sustained level.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 208.4 Tm (Explanation: When pressure stays high for days, the baroreceptors reset and treat the new level as normal, so they) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.3 Tm (defend it instead of correcting it. Long-term pressure is set mainly by the kidneys' control of salt and water.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 179.2 Tm (Not B: Unbounded sensitivity would strongly oppose the persistent rise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 165.8 Tm (Not C: Sensory fibres do not become motor fibres.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 152.4 Tm (Not D: Resetting does not require loss of every afferent signal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.0 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q44 | 18, 24-27) Tj ET
