q .08 .14 .26 rg 0 792 595 50 re f Q
BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 03 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (49. A patient with raised intracranial pressure develops hypertension and bradycardia together. What best) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (explains the slow pulse?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: C. The arterial baroreflex reacts to the sympathetic pressure rise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Medullary ischaemia first raises sympathetic pressure; arterial baroreceptors then increase vagal) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (drive and slow the heart.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: beta1 stimulation would usually accelerate, not slow, the heart.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not B: Cardiac autonomic signalling remains central to the response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: Atrial volume receptors respond chiefly to filling, not this sequence.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q55 | 32-36) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (50. Blood flow to the medullary vasomotor centre becomes severely reduced. Which local chemical change) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.0 Tm (directly activates its sympathetic response?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.3 Tm (Answer: C. Hypoxia together with accumulating carbon dioxide.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: Poor medullary perfusion produces hypoxia and hypercapnia, which strongly stimulate the vasomotor) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.9 Tm (centre to raise pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not A: High oxygen and low CO2 do not represent medullary ischaemia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Not B: Low acidity and abundant oxygen oppose the described stimulus.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.0 Tm (Not D: Adequate oxygen delivery would not produce the ischaemic response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.6 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q52 | 30-32) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (51. A patient has a large infarct affecting the lateral surface of one cerebral hemisphere. Which arterial) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 452.7 Tm (territory is most likely?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 436.0 Tm (Answer: C. Middle cerebral artery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 419.7 Tm (Explanation: The middle cerebral artery supplies most of the lateral cerebral hemisphere, making it the relevant) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 406.6 Tm (territory.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.5 Tm (Not A: The anterior cerebral artery mainly supplies medial frontal and parietal surfaces.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.1 Tm (Not B: The posterior cerebral artery predominantly supplies occipital and inferior temporal territories.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 363.7 Tm (Not D: The communicating artery is a collateral link rather than the main lateral supply.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 350.3 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q2 | 2-4; Chapter 22 ?2) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.1 Tm (52. Blood ejected from the right ventricle returns to the left atrium through which sequence?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 310.4 Tm (Answer: C. Pulmonary artery -> lung capillaries -> pulmonary veins.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.1 Tm (Explanation: The right ventricle pumps blood through pulmonary arteries to lung capillaries; pulmonary veins return) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.0 Tm (it to the left atrium.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.9 Tm (Not A: The aorta receives systemic output from the left ventricle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.5 Tm (Not B: That is mainly the systemic circulation after left ventricular ejection.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.1 Tm (Not D: Pulmonary veins carry blood into the left atrium rather than away from the right ventricle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q1 | 2-6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (53. Arterial pressure increases suddenly and baroreceptor firing rises. What changes first in central reflex) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 187.8 Tm (control?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.1 Tm (Answer: A. NTS activation increases depressor and vagal signalling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.8 Tm (Explanation: More stretch -> faster baroreceptor firing -> more input to the NTS. The NTS excites the depressor) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 141.7 Tm (area \(which inhibits the pressor area\) and the vagal neurons, so sympathetic output falls and vagal output rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.6 Tm (Not B: The heart remains under autonomic regulation during the reflex.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.2 Tm (Not C: The reflex suppresses rather than increases sympathetic pressor drive.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 98.8 Tm (Not D: Increased wall stretch increases, rather than decreases, afferent traffic.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 85.4 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q40 | 22-24) Tj ET
