BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 03 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (54. A patient with severe tricuspid regurgitation has a prominent systolic jugular venous wave. Which wave) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (becomes especially large?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. v wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Tricuspid regurgitation sends systolic flow back into the right atrium and systemic veins, producing a) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (large v or fused c-v wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: The a wave reflects atrial contraction and is not the characteristic regurgitant finding.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: The x descent may be blunted rather than unusually enlarged.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: The y descent is an emptying phase and does not represent systolic backflow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q71 | 26; Chapter 11 ?7 \(corrected physiology\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (55. A sudden increase in sympathetic venous tone can support cardiac output mainly through which immediate) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.0 Tm (effect?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.3 Tm (Answer: D. Venoconstriction shifts stored blood centrally.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: Sympathetic venoconstriction reduces the capacity of the venous reservoir and shifts stored blood) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.9 Tm (toward the heart, supporting venous return and end-diastolic filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not A: Systemic arterioles have no valves that close with sympathetic stimulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Not B: Venodilation generally increases peripheral capacitance and pooling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.0 Tm (Not C: Venoconstriction redistributes existing blood rather than rapidly generating plasma.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.6 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q73 | 73-75, 97-98; Chapter 12 ?2) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (56. What most clearly differentiates myocardial infarction from brief stable angina?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: A. Prolonged ischaemia can produce myocyte necrosis.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: Prolonged severe ischaemia can cause irreversible myocardial cell death; transient angina does not) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (necessarily cause necrosis.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not B: Infarction can occur across a range of heart rates.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not C: Transient angina ordinarily reflects reversible myocardial ischaemia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not D: Stable angina may result from fixed stenosis without rupture.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q28 | 29) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (57. A patient's heart rate is 80 beats/min and stroke volume is 75 mL. What is cardiac output?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 324.1 Tm (Answer: B. 6.0 L/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 307.8 Tm (Explanation: Cardiac output equals heart rate x stroke volume = 80 x 75 = 6000 mL/min or 6.0 L/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 291.7 Tm (Not A: 4.8 L/min would correspond to 60 mL at 80 beats/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.3 Tm (Not C: 7.5 L/min exceeds the calculated output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.9 Tm (Not D: 8.0 L/min assumes 100 mL per beat.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.5 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q34 | 35-36) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.3 Tm (58. What helps maintain forward systemic arterial flow between heartbeats?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 211.6 Tm (Answer: B. The elastic recoil of large arteries sustains diastolic flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.3 Tm (Explanation: Elastic arteries stretch during systole and recoil during diastole, smoothing pulsatile ejection into) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 182.2 Tm (continuous downstream flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 166.1 Tm (Not A: Venous valves prevent backflow but do not create aortic pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 152.7 Tm (Not C: Respiratory movement mainly assists venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.3 Tm (Not D: Capillaries lack the pumping muscle required to drive systemic flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q49 | 55-57) Tj ET
